Native Windows evidence and decision

Run 32493403293 disproved two assumptions from 7335d2ea. The portable Python fixtures now started correctly, but both descendant-cleanup tests left the child alive for their full 30 seconds. The root cause was the Windows process_exists authority: OpenProcess succeeds for an exited process object while another handle remains, so the PID-reuse guard treated the completed root as a live replacement and deliberately skipped cleanup.

The Windows authority now calls GetExitCodeProcess and reports true only for STILL_ACTIVE. This remains fail closed: an unreadable process or failed native query does not authorize descendant cleanup, and a reused live PID still blocks it. A Windows-only regression test keeps the Rust Child handle open after wait and requires process_exists to report false.

The attempted Windows resolver test in 7335d2ea also failed because it depended on the runner preinstalled Python rather than the controlled SearXNG recipe. That false proof is removed. resolve_with_path and label are Unix fake-executable fixture seams and are now compiled only with cfg(all(test, unix)); production Windows resolve remains compiled and checked, without dead-code allowances.

Executed locally with Rust 1.98.0: cargo fmt --check; cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings; SearXNG 85 passed and 1 release-wheelhouse test ignored; owned-process tests 3 passed on macOS; graphify update completed. The new Windows liveness test and descendant cleanup require the native PR runner, and no Windows success is claimed before it is green.
